#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093060} :activity/id #uuid "2179f3df-5754-599f-a160-da910b72c2ff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048712 :topic/id #uuid "b784cbd9-6f0d-5689-9a0e-6e19dfca34c7" :topic/math-academy-id 931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093431 :tutorial/content "Consider once again the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square that is spanned by these vectors.\n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/8f/8fca8cf6dd5b232d3aed6eeb98fb6c244872c55425673cf3ac1441a22e2e29f3.svg) \n\nAs before, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/3f/3f8b5f0f827d359f32a4311021dc48947447133918d48a2f1cad511531b13c81.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/41/4195c8cd2f22fb0f84056226fb7b28c5f9944f747ca3230bf187752829b2a04e.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "70a75b2e-3fa9-5aa9-b308-79b56d57fae7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3542 :tutorial/title "Rotations of 90 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "f91bce30-93ef-5342-8485-0619a059a2b3" :step/math-academy-id 20041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092065 :question/id #uuid "a74be952-4dd4-5a7f-a8a4-de68977c5bb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5457"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420105}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "7452acd6-f07a-5cbb-a6a1-887429d397a8" :step/math-academy-id 20045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093432 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/c6/c6865c5373231451885b3d76e3c8151e9c707462a8b405d9b014b1bda17065a8.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n![](images/5b/5b8e1c8e03ebb60716eaa2fefded5e7fc286f6de9cffdece8200f6f56c2bbe8e.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c2dc9f76bb23225cec0a4f62541d8a74c4c4b16d2e2f7c37e8d36922d97c59f0.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) about the origin.**\n\nNote that we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "410fc9b7-ab2d-52ba-9b26-7afd34fdf9e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3545 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "68d9dc46-2592-5260-a1bb-53e6b885e53f" :step/math-academy-id 20046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092066 :question/id #uuid "e945271e-2180-559b-9998-53ab9982043c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5458"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050589}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354275}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "9915017c-1452-5136-bb94-65860a727a47" :step/math-academy-id 20047 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093058}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093434 :tutorial/content "Finally, let \n\n![](images/1b/1b840cbdb5b3953dfef9910d34248ea280bf38299e4f0a3d0a78143e7301f159.svg)\n\nbe a matrix of a linear transformation ![](images/2d/2d90be63023ff97e21e1a97ccee6c387437ac2a99787278c6f71f37e3ac86850.svg) As usual, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/76/76e24611a2cd8a3d5d01fc0a04a9b7a3d0957db84dd1a45bc2f1f82492fb2efd.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0f/0fc5f33d0bd7047ab02bd9bc3ea3b00c083ab214f3bc8a51e9cbb6e54dc6b51b.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "49541094-17cb-52e7-bb58-8ee2199ad6d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3547 :tutorial/title "Rotations of 270 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "d48d44d1-fe67-587c-a56e-251eee6b68f8" :step/math-academy-id 20053 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093135}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 